  "Name": "Harrick1970-TableI-p",
  "Source": "N. J. Harrick and A. F. Turner, \"A Thin Film Optical Cavity to Induce Absorption or Thermal Emission,\" Appl. Opt. 9, 2111-2114 (1970). doi:10.1364/AO.9.002111 - Table I (p design), p. 2112; construction Fig. 1, p. 2111",
  "Notes": "Cavity inducing complete p-polarized absorption in a weak absorber (n0 = 1.4 - i0.04) from a Si hemicylinder by internal reflection\nSpecification: Rp = 0 (complete absorption A = 1 - R = 1 in the thick absorbing layer) at alpha = 28.8 deg in the Si hemicylinder, p polarization.\nReported: Table I p design: Rp = 0.002, Rs = 0.998 at alpha = 28.8 deg (at the design wavelength, where thicknesses are h/lambda = 0.124 cavity, 0.260 FTR). For comparison (text p. 2112): absorber in direct contact with the Si hemicylinder, no films, gives Rp = 0.82 and Rs = 0.93.\nNotes: Table I gives physical thicknesses as h/lambda with wavelength-independent indices, so the design scales with lambda; reference_nm = 1000 is chosen so that thickness in nm = 1000 x h/lambda exactly (0.124 -> 124 nm, 0.260 -> 260 nm). The paper scaled it to the 3.4 um region for the experiment (Fig. 2: 3.3 um C-H band in chloroform, measured at 29.5 deg). Paper lists from the absorber side (n0 absorber, n1 cavity, n2 FTR, n hemicylinder); reversed here so layer 1 (FTR SiO2) is next to the incident Si hemicylinder. Light is incident from the Si (n = 3.5) at alpha = 28.8 deg in the Si, beyond the 24.5 deg critical angle of the SiO2 film. Paper's index convention is n - ik; k = 0.04 positive for absorption. Absorber taken as semi-infinite substrate (thickness infinity in Table I).\nLayers from Table I (p design), p. 2112; construction Fig. 1, p. 2111; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours Rp 0.002, Rs 0.998 at 28.8 deg, 1000 nm; paper Rp 0.002, Rs 0.998 (Tables I and III).",
